  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "A tongue sticking out of a mouth 口"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a tongue sticking out of a mouth. In old scripts written the same as 舌 but with an extra line."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "直言曰言，論難曰語。从口䇂聲。凡言之屬皆从言。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Ideogram (指事) – Early oracle bone script: 口 (“mouth”) + ▽ (possibly original form of 弋, marking location). Later: 舌 (“tongue”) (mouth with a snake's tongue) + ▽, sharing strokes at the tip. By the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, a horizontal stroke was added above to indicate the source of speech or the words spoken.\nSee also 音.\nShuowen erroneously analyzes the glyph as a phono-semantic compound (形聲 /形声): phonetic 䇂 (qiān) + semantic 口 (“mouth”).\nAccording to Schuessler (2007: 74) this word is derived from 語 (OC *ŋaʔ, “to speak”) + -n nominal suffix."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "直言曰言，論難曰語。从口䇂聲。凡言之屬皆从言。",
  "shuowenExplanation": "straight, words, debate, difficult, language. Composed of 口 \"mouth\" (semantic) and 䇂 \"(ancient form) fault\" (phonetic). All characters in the 言 \"words\" category use this as radical.",
